 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 5.04 — Symmetric Spaces\n\n5.03 produced $S^n$, $H^n$, $\\mathbb{CP}^n$ and the Grassmannians as quotients $G/H$. They have more in common than that.\n\n**The definition.** A Riemannian manifold $M$ is **symmetric** if for every $p \\in M$ there is an isometry $s_p$ with\n\n$$s_p(p) = p, \\qquad ds_p|_p = -\\mathrm{id}.$$\n\nIt fixes $p$ and reverses every direction, so it reverses every geodesic through $p$. On $\\mathbb{E}^n$ this is $x \\mapsto 2p - x$; on $S^n$ it is reflection through the axis $\\pm p$; on $H^n$, inversion in the point.\n\n**Cartan's algebraic reformulation.** Such an $M$ is $G/H$ with an involution $\\sigma$ of $G$ ($\\sigma^2 = \\mathrm{id}$) whose fixed set is $H$. Differentiating $\\sigma$ splits the algebra into $\\pm1$ eigenspaces,\n\n$$\\mathfrak g = \\mathfrak h \\oplus \\mathfrak m, \\qquad d\\sigma|_{\\mathfrak h} = +1,\\quad d\\sigma|_{\\mathfrak m} = -1,$$\n\nand because $\\sigma$ is an automorphism the brackets are forced:\n\n$$[\\mathfrak h,\\mathfrak h] \\subseteq \\mathfrak h, \\qquad [\\mathfrak h,\\mathfrak m]\\subseteq\\mathfrak m, \\qquad [\\mathfrak m,\\mathfrak m]\\subseteq\\mathfrak h.$$\n\nThe third inclusion is the whole point. $\\mathfrak m \\cong T_pM$ is *not* a subalgebra — the bracket of two tangent directions lands back in the isotropy algebra, and that is what curvature is:\n\n$$R(X,Y)Z = -[[X,Y],Z], \\qquad X,Y,Z \\in \\mathfrak m.$$\n\n**Curvature with no derivatives at all.** No Christoffel symbols, no metric in coordinates. And $\\nabla R = 0$: the curvature tensor is parallel, which is an equivalent characterisation of symmetric spaces.\n\n**Duality.** Given $\\mathfrak g = \\mathfrak h \\oplus \\mathfrak m$, form\n\n$$\\mathfrak g^* = \\mathfrak h \\oplus i\\mathfrak m.$$\n\nThe bracket relations survive, but $[\\mathfrak m,\\mathfrak m] \\subseteq \\mathfrak h$ picks up a sign — so the curvature flips. **Every compact symmetric space has a non-compact dual and vice versa**, paired by $K \\leftrightarrow -K$:\n\n| compact | non-compact |\n|---|---|\n| $S^n = SO(n+1)/SO(n)$ | $H^n = SO(n,1)/SO(n)$ |\n| $\\mathbb{CP}^n = SU(n+1)/S(U(n){\\times}U(1))$ | $\\mathbb{CH}^n = SU(n,1)/S(U(n){\\times}U(1))$ |\n| $SU(2)$ | $SL_2(\\mathbb R)$ (as a symmetric space of its own type) |\n\nThe sphere and the hyperbolic plane of Stage 2 are not merely analogous. They are **dual**, in a sense that is a sign change in one bracket.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nbr(X, Y) := ratsimp( X.Y - Y.X )$\n\n/* --- S² = SO(3)/SO(2), with the involution σ(g) = SgS --- */\nS3 : matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,-1])$\nsig(X) := ratsimp( S3 . X . S3 )$\nLb : [ matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]),\n       matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[-1,0,0]),\n       matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0]) ]$\n\nprint(\"σ(X) = SXS with S = diag(1,1,−1), an involution of so(3):\")$\nprint(\"   σ² = id ?  σ(σ(L₁)) − L₁ = \", sig(sig(Lb[1])) - Lb[1])$\nfor k : 1 thru 3 do\n  print(\"   σ(L\", k, \") = \", sig(Lb[k]),\n        \"   eigenvalue \", if sig(Lb[k]) = Lb[k] then \"+1\" else \"-1\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   so  h = span(L₃)  (the rotations fixing the north pole: SO(2))\")$\nprint(\"       m = span(L₁, L₂)  ≅ T_pS²\")$\n\nhb : [ Lb[3] ]$\nmb : [ Lb[1], Lb[2] ]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the three bracket relations:\")$\nprint(\"   [h,h] : [L₃,L₃] = \", br(hb[1],hb[1]), \"  ⊆ h ✓\")$\nprint(\"   [h,m] : [L₃,L₁] = \", br(hb[1],mb[1]), \" = L₂ ∈ m ✓\")$\nprint(\"           [L₃,L₂] = \", br(hb[1],mb[2]), \" = −L₁ ∈ m ✓\")$\nprint(\"   [m,m] : [L₁,L₂] = \", br(mb[1],mb[2]), \" = L₃ ∈ h ✓\")$\nprint(\"   The last one is the point: m is NOT a subalgebra.  The bracket of\")$\nprint(\"   two tangent directions lands in the isotropy algebra, and that\")$\nprint(\"   failure to close is the curvature.\")$\n\n/* --- curvature R(X,Y)Z = −[[X,Y],Z] --- */\nipso(X, Y) := ratsimp( -(1/2) * mat_trace(X . Y) )$    /* Lᵢ orthonormal */\nRsym(X, Y, Z) := ratsimp( -br( br(X,Y), Z) )$\nKsym(X, Y, ipf) := ratsimp( ipf(Rsym(X,Y,Y), X)\n                            / ( ipf(X,X)*ipf(Y,Y) - ipf(X,Y)^2 ) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"metric ⟨X,Y⟩ = −½tr(XY) on m:  Gram = \",\n      genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ipso(mb[i], mb[j])), 2, 2))$\nprint(\"   R(L₁,L₂)L₂ = −[[L₁,L₂],L₂] = \", Rsym(mb[1],mb[2],mb[2]))$\nprint(\"   K = ⟨R(X,Y)Y, X⟩ / (|X|²|Y|² − ⟨X,Y⟩²) = \",\n      Ksym(mb[1], mb[2], ipso))$\nprint(\"   K = 1: the unit 2-sphere.  Computed with no coordinates, no\")$\nprint(\"   Christoffel symbols and no differentiation — only brackets.\")$\n\n/* the geodesic symmetry itself */\nsp : -S3$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the geodesic symmetry at the north pole e₃ is s_p = \", sp)$\nprint(\"   det = \", determinant(sp), \"  (so it lies in SO(3), not just O(3))\")$\nprint(\"   s_p e₃ − e₃ = \", sp . transpose(matrix([0,0,1]))\n                           - transpose(matrix([0,0,1])))$\nprint(\"   s_p e₁ + e₁ = \", sp . transpose(matrix([1,0,0]))\n                           + transpose(matrix([1,0,0])),\n      \"   (so ds_p = −id on T_p) ✓\")$\nprint(\"   s_p = −S, and conjugation by −S is the same as conjugation by S,\")$\nprint(\"   so the involution of the GROUP and the symmetry of the SPACE are\")$\nprint(\"   the same map in disguise.  That is Cartan's correspondence.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "σ(X) = SXS with S = diag(1,1,−1), an involution of so(3):\n   σ² = id ?  σ(σ(L₁)) − L₁ =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   σ(L 1 ) =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,-1,0])    eigenvalue  -1\n   σ(L 2 ) =  matrix([0,0,-1],[0,0,0],[1,0,0])    eigenvalue  -1\n   σ(L 3 ) =  matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0])    eigenvalue  +1\n   so  h = span(L₃)  (the rotations fixing the north pole: SO(2))\n       m = span(L₁, L₂)  ≅ T_pS²\nthe three bracket relations:\n   [h,h] : [L₃,L₃] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])   ⊆ h ✓\n   [h,m] : [L₃,L₁] =  matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[-1,0,0])  = L₂ ∈ m ✓\n           [L₃,L₂] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,-1,0])  = −L₁ ∈ m ✓\n   [m,m] : [L₁,L₂] =  matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0])  = L₃ ∈ h ✓\n   The last one is the point: m is NOT a subalgebra.  The bracket of\n   two tangent directions lands in the isotropy algebra, and that\n   failure to close is the curvature.\nmetric ⟨X,Y⟩ = −½tr(XY) on m:  Gram =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])\n   R(L₁,L₂)L₂ = −[[L₁,L₂],L₂] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0])\n   K = ⟨R(X,Y)Y, X⟩ / (|X|²|Y|² − ⟨X,Y⟩²) =  1\n   K = 1: the unit 2-sphere.  Computed with no coordinates, no\n   Christoffel symbols and no differentiation — only brackets.\nthe geodesic symmetry at the north pole e₃ is s_p =\n  matrix([-1,0,0],[0,-1,0],[0,0,1])\n   det =  1   (so it lies in SO(3), not just O(3))\n   s_p e₃ − e₃ =  matrix([0],[0],[0])\n   s_p e₁ + e₁ =  matrix([0],[0],[0])    (so ds_p = −id on T_p) ✓\n   s_p = −S, and conjugation by −S is the same as conjugation by S,\n   so the involution of the GROUP and the symmetry of the SPACE are\n   the same map in disguise.  That is Cartan's correspondence."
     ]
    }
