  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The dual: H² = SO(2,1)/SO(2) --- */\nJm : matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,-1])$\nB1 : matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[1,0,0])$\nB2 : matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,1,0])$\nprint(\"so(2,1) = {X : XᵀJ + JX = 0} with J = diag(1,1,−1):\")$\nfor M in [Lb[3], B1, B2] do\n  print(\"   X = \", M, \"   XᵀJ + JX = \", transpose(M).Jm + Jm.M)$\n\nhb2 : [ Lb[3] ]$\nmb2 : [ B1, B2 ]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   h = span(L₃) = so(2) again;  m = span(B₁, B₂), the boosts.\")$\nprint(\"   [h,m] : [L₃,B₁] = \", br(Lb[3],B1), \" = B₂ ∈ m ✓\")$\nprint(\"   [m,m] : [B₁,B₂] = \", br(B1,B2), \" = −L₃ ∈ h ✓\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Compare the sphere: [L₁,L₂] = +L₃.  Here [B₁,B₂] = −L₃.\")$\nprint(\"   ONE SIGN.  That is the entire difference between the two\")$\nprint(\"   geometries at the level of the algebra.\")$\n\nipm(X, Y) := ratsimp( (1/2) * mat_trace(X . Y) )$   /* +½tr on the boosts */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"metric ⟨X,Y⟩ = +½tr(XY) on m:  Gram = \",\n      genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ipm(mb2[i], mb2[j])), 2, 2), \" ✓\")$\nprint(\"   R(B₁,B₂)B₂ = \", Rsym(B1,B2,B2))$\nprint(\"   K = \", Ksym(B1, B2, ipm), \"   — the hyperbolic plane.\")$\nprint(\"   Same formula R(X,Y)Z = −[[X,Y],Z], same metric normalisation,\")$\nprint(\"   opposite answer, because one bracket changed sign.\")$\n\n/* --- the duality map, explicitly --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"THE DUALITY g* = h ⊕ i·m, on the nose:\")$\nprint(\"   [iL₁, iL₂] = \", ratsimp( br(%i*Lb[1], %i*Lb[2]) ),\n      \" = −L₃,  matching [B₁,B₂] = \", br(B1,B2))$\nprint(\"   [L₃, iL₁]  = \", ratsimp( br(Lb[3], %i*Lb[1]) ),\n      \" = iL₂,  matching [L₃,B₁] = \", br(Lb[3],B1), \" = B₂\")$\nprint(\"   So  L₃ ↦ L₃,  iL₁ ↦ B₁,  iL₂ ↦ B₂  respects every bracket:\")$\nprint(\"   so(3) and so(2,1) are two REAL FORMS of the same complex algebra.\")$\nprint(\"   Multiplying m by i sends the compact form to the non-compact one,\")$\nprint(\"   and K to −K.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   S² and H² are not analogues.  They are duals.  2.07 put them\")$\nprint(\"   side by side as 'the three geometries'; this is the reason they\")$\nprint(\"   belong side by side.\")$\n\n/* --- and the flat case sits between --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   The flat case E² = E(2)/SO(2) is the DEGENERATE member: there\")$\nprint(\"   [m,m] = 0 exactly, so R ≡ 0.  The three Erlangen geometries are\")$\nprint(\"   [m,m] ⊆ h with a plus sign, with zero, and with a minus sign.\")$\nTb : [ matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[0,0,0]), matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,0,0]) ]$\nprint(\"   E(2) as 3×3 matrices, m = translations: [T₁,T₂] = \",\n      br(Tb[1], Tb[2]), \"  ⟹ R = \", Rsym(Tb[1],Tb[2],Tb[2]), \" ✓\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "so(2,1) = {X : XᵀJ + JX = 0} with J = diag(1,1,−1):\n   X =  matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0])    XᵀJ + JX =\n       matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   X =  matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[1,0,0])    XᵀJ + JX =\n       matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   X =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,1,0])    XᵀJ + JX =\n       matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   h = span(L₃) = so(2) again;  m = span(B₁, B₂), the boosts.\n   [h,m] : [L₃,B₁] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,1,0])  = B₂ ∈ m ✓\n   [m,m] : [B₁,B₂] =  matrix([0,1,0],[-1,0,0],[0,0,0])  = −L₃ ∈ h ✓\n   Compare the sphere: [L₁,L₂] = +L₃.  Here [B₁,B₂] = −L₃.\n   ONE SIGN.  That is the entire difference between the two\n   geometries at the level of the algebra.\nmetric ⟨X,Y⟩ = +½tr(XY) on m:  Gram =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])  ✓\n   R(B₁,B₂)B₂ =  matrix([0,0,-1],[0,0,0],[-1,0,0])\n   K =  -1    — the hyperbolic plane.\n   Same formula R(X,Y)Z = −[[X,Y],Z], same metric normalisation,\n   opposite answer, because one bracket changed sign.\nTHE DUALITY g* = h ⊕ i·m, on the nose:\n   [iL₁, iL₂] =  matrix([0,1,0],[-1,0,0],[0,0,0])  = −L₃,  matching [B₁,B₂] =\n                 matrix([0,1,0],[-1,0,0],[0,0,0])\n   [L₃, iL₁]  =  matrix([0,0,%i],[0,0,0],[-%i,0,0])\n                 = iL₂,  matching [L₃,B₁] =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,1],[0,1,0])\n                 = B₂\n   So  L₃ ↦ L₃,  iL₁ ↦ B₁,  iL₂ ↦ B₂  respects every bracket:\n   so(3) and so(2,1) are two REAL FORMS of the same complex algebra.\n   Multiplying m by i sends the compact form to the non-compact one,\n   and K to −K.\n   S² and H² are not analogues.  They are duals.  2.07 put them\n   side by side as 'the three geometries'; this is the reason they\n   belong side by side.\n   The flat case E² = E(2)/SO(2) is the DEGENERATE member: there\n   [m,m] = 0 exactly, so R ≡ 0.  The three Erlangen geometries are\n   [m,m] ⊆ h with a plus sign, with zero, and with a minus sign.\n   E(2) as 3×3 matrices, m = translations: [T₁,T₂] =\n  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])   ⟹ R =  matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,0],[0,0,0])  ✓"
     ]
    }
