   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\mathfrak g = \\mathfrak h \\oplus \\mathfrak m, \\qquad [\\mathfrak m,\\mathfrak m]\\subseteq\\mathfrak h, \\qquad R(X,Y)Z = -[[X,Y],Z]$$\n\n**Cartan's decomposition, computed.** For $S^2 = SO(3)/SO(2)$ the involution $\\sigma(X) = SXS$ with $S = \\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-1)$ splits $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ into $\\mathfrak h = \\mathrm{span}(L_3)$ (eigenvalue $+1$) and $\\mathfrak m = \\mathrm{span}(L_1,L_2)$ (eigenvalue $-1$), and all three bracket relations came out as stated. The one that matters is $[L_1,L_2] = L_3 \\in \\mathfrak h$: **$\\mathfrak m$ is not a subalgebra**, and its failure to close *is* the curvature.\n\n$K = 1$ then followed from $R(X,Y)Z = -[[X,Y],Z]$ with $\\langle X,Y\\rangle = -\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}(XY)$ — no coordinates, no Christoffel symbols, no differentiation of anything.\n\nThe geodesic symmetry itself is $s_p = \\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,1)$, verified to fix $e_3$, to act as $-\\mathrm{id}$ on the tangent plane, and to have determinant $1$ so it lies in $SO(3)$. It equals $-S$, and conjugation by $-S$ is conjugation by $S$ — the group involution and the space symmetry are one map.\n\n**One sign is the whole difference.**\n\n| | $[\\mathfrak m,\\mathfrak m]$ | $K$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| $S^2 = SO(3)/SO(2)$ | $[L_1,L_2] = +L_3$ | $+1$ |\n| $\\mathbb{E}^2 = E(2)/SO(2)$ | $[T_1,T_2] = 0$ | $0$ |\n| $H^2 = SO(2,1)/SO(2)$ | $[B_1,B_2] = -L_3$ | $-1$ |\n\nSame formula, same metric normalisation, three answers — driven by one bracket. And the duality map is explicit: $L_3 \\mapsto L_3$, $iL_1 \\mapsto B_1$, $iL_2 \\mapsto B_2$ respects every bracket, because $[iL_1,iL_2] = -L_3 = [B_1,B_2]$ and $[L_3,iL_1] = iL_2 \\mapsto B_2 = [L_3,B_1]$. $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ and $\\mathfrak{so}(2,1)$ are **two real forms of one complex algebra**; multiplying $\\mathfrak m$ by $i$ swaps compact for non-compact and $K$ for $-K$.\n\n2.07 set the three geometries side by side as a matter of presentation. This is why they belong there.\n\n**The curvature identities are the Lie algebra axioms.** For generic $X,Y,Z \\in \\mathfrak m$, the first Bianchi identity $R(X,Y)Z + R(Y,Z)X + R(Z,X)Y = 0$ came out identically zero — because it *is* the Jacobi identity with the outer bracket negated. 4.04 checked Bianchi component by component on a metric; here it is free.\n\n**Rank, and where flatness hides.** The rank is the dimension of a maximal abelian subspace of $\\mathfrak m$ — equivalently of a maximal flat totally geodesic submanifold.\n\n- $S^2$: $[L_1,L_2] \\ne 0$, so no 2-dimensional abelian subspace. **Rank 1.** True of every $S^n$ and $H^n$; rank 1 is what constant curvature looks like from here.\n- $Gr(2,4) = SO(4)/(SO(2)\\times SO(2))$: $\\dim\\mathfrak m = 4 = k(n-k)$, and the commutator table shows exactly two commuting pairs, $(M_{13},M_{24})$ and $(M_{14},M_{23})$ — disjoint index pairs. **Rank 2.**\n\nThe consequence is visible in the curvature: $K(M_{13},M_{24}) = 0$ while $K(M_{13},M_{14}) = K(M_{13},M_{23}) = 1$. A **flat 2-plane inside a compact symmetric space**. $Gr(2,4)$ has $K \\ge 0$ but not $K > 0$; it contains flat 2-tori. Rank 1 is precisely the case with no flats at all.\n\n## Next\n\n**5.05 — Principal Bundles and Connections** stops asking curvature to live on the base manifold. A connection becomes a $\\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form on a principal $G$-bundle, curvature becomes $F = dA + A\\wedge A$, and the Levi-Civita connection of 4.02 is one case among many — the case $G = SO(n)$ acting on the frame bundle. It is also, with $G = U(1)$, electromagnetism.\n\n## References\n\n- Helgason, S. (1978) *Differential Geometry, Lie Groups, and Symmetric Spaces*. New York: Academic Press. Chapters 4–5 are the definitive account: the Cartan decomposition, the curvature formula, duality, and the full classification into types I–IV.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9). Chapter 3 for the symmetric-space examples at this level, before the machinery.\n- Besse, A.L. (1987) *Einstein Manifolds*. Berlin: Springer. Chapter 7 §F tabulates the irreducible symmetric spaces with their ranks and dimensions — the table this notebook computes two rows of.\n- Eberlein, P. (1996) *Geometry of Nonpositively Curved Manifolds*. Chicago: University of Chicago Press. For the non-compact side, where rank becomes the organising invariant.\n- Cartan, É. (1926, 1927) 'Sur une classe remarquable d'espaces de Riemann', *Bulletin de la Société Mathématique de France*, 54, pp. 214–264 and 55, pp. 114–134. The original classification. Its method — reduce the geometry to an involution of a Lie algebra — is exactly what this notebook carries out on two examples.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. The rank-2 claim for $Gr(2,4)$ rests on the printed commutator table: two pairs commute, every other pair does not, so no 3-dimensional abelian subspace can be built from this basis.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
